# Admin Dashboard Styling — Handoff Notes

**Scope:** Bring the admin dashboard's visual design in line with the existing
student dashboard (`dashboard.js` / `dashboard.css`), mobile-first, sharing
the app's existing `scrollEffects.js` utility rather than duplicating it.

**Files touched:** `admin.css` (new), `admin.js` (updated), `scrollEffects.js`
(comment + one breakpoint fix only — logic untouched).

---

## 1. Design reference

The student dashboard (`dashboard.css`) was used as the source of truth for
the visual language:

- Purple gradient hero: `linear-gradient(135deg, #7c3aed, #5b21b6)`
- Card radii: 20–24px, `box-shadow: 0 8px 30px rgba(0,0,0,0.05)`, `1px solid
  #ececec` borders
- Entrance motion: `.load-in` (pop-in on mount) and `.reveal` /
  `.reveal-visible` (scroll-triggered), both driven by `scrollEffects.js`

## 2. `admin.css` — what was built

- Gradient hero header (`.admin-header`), tab bar (`.tab-btn` / `.active`),
  overview stat cards (`.overview-grid` / `.overview-card`), and activity
  list — styled to match the student dashboard's card/shadow/radius language.
- Own `admin-pop-in` `@keyframes` (kept distinct from the student dashboard's
  `dashboard-pop-in`, even though the class name applying it — `.load-in` —
  is the same).

## 3. `admin.js` — what changed

- Added `.admin-wrap`, `data-reveal`, and `data-parallax-speed` hooks to the
  markup.
- Imports `initParallax` / `initScrollReveal` from `scrollEffects.js` and
  calls them after every tab render.
- Re-runs those on each dashboard-tab render specifically, since that tab
  injects its own `.reveal` / `data-parallax-speed` nodes into the DOM.
- Reuses the existing stale-async guard pattern (`if (activeTab !==
  "dashboard") return;` in `loadDashboardStats`) — nothing new here, just
  confirming it's still in place and still correct.

## 4. Bug caught and fixed: cross-page class collision

**Problem:** `.load-in`, `.reveal`, and `.reveal-visible` were identical
class *names* in both `admin.css` and `dashboard.css`. Since both
stylesheets load together in the SPA, whichever one loaded last would
silently win for **every** `.load-in`/`.reveal` element on **either** page —
not just its own.

**Fix:** Every rule in `admin.css` is scoped under `.admin-wrap` (e.g.
`.admin-wrap .load-in`, `.admin-wrap .reveal`), which raises specificity
enough to keep the two pages isolated regardless of load order. No changes
were needed to `scrollEffects.js`'s `data-reveal` / `reveal-visible`
contract — it still just toggles a class name, and now each page's own CSS
governs what that class means.

## 5. Mobile-first rebuild

Given most users are on phones, phone is the *default* layout, not a
shrink-to-fit fallback:

- Tab bar (`.admin-tabs`) is `position: sticky` and scrolls horizontally
  (scrollbar hidden) instead of wrapping into cramped multi-line labels.
- Logout button is full-width, 44px min-height (thumb-friendly tap target)
  on phone; becomes an inline button at `600px+`.
- Overview stats stay a 2-column grid even on the smallest phones (legible
  at a glance, not one long scrolling column) — 3 columns at `600px+`, 6 at
  `768px+`.
- Padding, font sizes, and radii are all trimmed by default and stepped up
  via `min-width: 600px` and `min-width: 768px` queries.

## 6. `scrollEffects.js` — reviewed, not duplicated

It's intentionally page-agnostic (re-queries the DOM fresh every scroll
tick, one shared listener, selector-driven via `data-reveal` /
`data-parallax-speed`) — so admin didn't need its own copy. A dedicated
admin version would've reintroduced the same class-collision risk described
in §4.

Two small edits only:

- Header comment now lists the admin dashboard as a third consumer
  (previously said "landing page and student dashboard" only).
- `initParallax()` turns parallax off below `768px`, but `admin.css` was
  originally switching to desktop tab layout at `900px` — a mismatch where
  hero shapes could start drifting while the tab bar was still in mobile
  mode. Both are now aligned to `768px`.

## 7. Current file location

The shared utility lives at `student/scrollEffects.js` and `admin.js`
imports it from there directly (`../student/scrollEffects.js`) — this is
correct and works fine functionally.

**Open suggestion (not urgent):** since landing, student, and admin all
depend on it equally, consider relocating it to a neutral path like
`shared/scrollEffects.js` at some point, so the folder name doesn't imply
student ownership of a cross-app utility. Purely organizational — no
behavior change.

---

## Outstanding / not yet done

- **`animateCountUp` on overview stats** — the student dashboard count-up's
  its purchased-quiz number on load; the admin overview stats (`Subjects`,
  `Weekly Quizzes`, `Questions`, `Students`, `Total Revenue`, `Top Selling
  Quiz`) currently just snap from `--` to their final value once
  `loadDashboardStats()` resolves. Wiring `animateCountUp` in would bring
  parity, and the piece it needs (`animateCountUp` export) already exists in
  `scrollEffects.js` — not yet done, flagged for whoever picks this up next.

## Files in this handoff

- `admin.css`
- `admin.js`
- `scrollEffects.js` (comment + breakpoint fix)